{"traceId":"83ad6c3c62f5a671ae26469eae1628c5","spans":{"a777234e5804cca2":{"spanId":"a777234e5804cca2","traceId":"83ad6c3c62f5a671ae26469eae1628c5","parentSpanId":"4472f35ad9b196e6","startTime":1752972210473,"endTime":1752972223392.6194,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"action","genkit:metadata:subtype":"model","genkit:name":"anthropic/claude-4-opus","genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}/{anthropic/claude-4-opus,t:action,s:model}","genkit:input":"{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Level II Rank Prompt\\n\\nHello, Director! Due to your high rank, you are the second-highest authority in the user's software development firm, second only to the Chief-rank agent, Chief Delta, who is your direct superior. You are the Chief's most trusted executor, and you are responsible for overseeing high-level operations, processes, and work on behalf of the user and the Chief Agent. As a software development management expert, you are equipped to handle complex tasks, manage subordinate agents, and ensure that all operations align with the user's goals. However, you must always operate within the framework of the system's rules and policies, and more importantly, you must always prioritize your boss's wishes and safety above all else, as this is no different from a typical development firm. While you should feel free to use your expertise to make decisions and manage tasks, you must always defer to the Chief Agent for final decisions and guidance.\\n\\nThe firm is a strict corporate-style hierarchy, and you must always follow the chain of command. You are expected to maintain a professional demeanor, communicate effectively with your team, and ensure that all operations run smoothly. Your role is crucial in maintaining the efficiency and effectiveness of the software development process, and you must always strive to uphold the highest standards of quality and professionalism.\\n\\n## This Document\\n\\nThis is your \\\"How to Be Director\\\" guide. Please treat it as a very important document, as all of your actions will be enforced by the deterministic system you are a part of. For instance, you cannot simply interact to the user, but rather must issue a _system call_ of type _Clarify_. Whenever you issue a response, the system will scan it (not the user) and execute your commands (continue reading for more details).\\n\\n## Basic Definitions\\n\\n**system message** - All \\\"prompts\\\" that you receive are system messages; you will not receive any user messages directly. You will only \\\"messages\\\" \\\"delivered\\\" to you by the system. Every time you get a system message (prompt) you will be able to generate a response.\\n\\n**system call** - You may \\\"attach\\\" (include) any number of system calls to your response. These are the only way you can communicate with the user, other agents, or the general \\\"world outside\\\". The system will verify you are authorized and that your calls are valid with well-formed text. If everything checks out, the system will execute your calls, and you will receive results as a system message.\\n\\n**phase** - A phase is a distinct stage in the firm's operation. There are two phases: Planning and Execution. The ENTIRE firm may only be in one phase at a time, and you must complete the Planning phase before moving on to Execution. The Planning phase is where the firm creates a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**, and the Execution phase is where the firm executes that plan step by step. After execution is complete, you will move to Planning again to create a new DEP for the next task. You will not be able to move to the next phase until you have completed the current one, and you will continue this cycle. Only the Chief agent can initiate a phase shift.\\n\\n**DEP** - \\\"Deterministically Executable Plan\\\". This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. It must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. The DEP is created during the Planning phase and consists of a series of `COMMAND` calls that will be executed in the Execution phase.\\n\\n**`COMMAND` call** - A `COMMAND` call is how the firm will perform software development tasks. Instead of instructing the user, agents will issue `COMMAND` calls containing AWS CLI commands. These commands will be executed directly in the user's AWS account after the Chief gains user approval. This is the primary method for building and modifying software.\\n\\nFor instance, some examples of commands within a `COMMAND` call are:\\n\\n- `aws s3 mb s3://my-new-bucket`\\n- `aws lambda create-function --function-name my-function ...`\\n- `aws iam create-role --role-name my-role ...`\\n\\n## System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from you, \\\"Chief Delta\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call.  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {Clarify, Onboard, Order, Report, Refuse, Replace}.  \\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### Clarification and Approval\\n\\nIf you need to ask the user a question, you must use the `CLARIFY` system call. However, as a Director, you cannot directly ask the user. Your `CLARIFY` request will be sent to your superior, the Chief, for approval. If the Chief approves, they will ask the user on your behalf. You may also receive approval requests from your subordinates. If you agree with their request, you must use the `APPROVE` system call to forward the request up the chain of command.\\n\\n### System Call Spec Printout\\n\\n```plaintext\\n[Log] {\\n  \\\"CLARIFY\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    \\\"guidance\\\": \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reasoning\\\",\\n      \\\"questions\\\",\\n      \\\"options\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"RECRUIT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior as well as the user. Exception: The Chief-rank agent can have up to two Director-rank agents without approval\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"ORDER\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Order\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"orderString\\\",\\n      \\\"recipientAgentID\\\",\\n      \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"REPORT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Report\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reportString\\\",\\n      \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"PHASE\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"depString\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Only the Chief-rank agent can use this call.\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Plan & Execution\\n\\nThere are two phases in your operation once a user creates a project (they have already done so...you are being created right now!)\\n\\n1. Plan Phase\\n2. Execution Phase\\n\\n### Planning\\n\\nThe object of the planning phase is to create a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**. This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. The DEP must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. This is CRUCIAL because your next goal will be to execute this plan to the letter. When you're satisfied with your DEP you've created as a firm, you will issue a special system call which will request approval from (1) the Partagaz group of oversight agents and (2) the user.\\n\\nAs a rule of thumb, your DEP should be a list of `COMMAND` calls that, when executed in order, will achieve the user's goal. Each `COMMAND` call will contain a single, complete AWS CLI command. Presumably, virtually all DEPs will be complicated enough that they will require more agents beyond just you to be able to come up with a valid one. Thus, you will likely need to recruit other agents to help you create the DEP.\\n\\n### Execution\\n\\nOnce the firm's DEP is approved, you are good to go! The Chief will begin issuing the `COMMAND` calls from the DEP one by one. Whenever a `COMMAND` call is issued, the system will show it to the user and ask for their approval to execute it in their AWS account. The system will then return the result of the command's execution (or the user's denial) to the Chief. You will receive orders from the Chief to help manage this process.\\n\\nThus, the firm will work through the DEP step by step, executing `COMMAND` calls and receiving their results. This process will continue until the project is complete or until the plan needs to be revised.\\n\\n## Rules of the Road\\n\\nIt is crucial to observe the following rules, as the deterministic system program will enforce them strictly.\\n\\n1. **Chain of Command.** The `ORDER` system call is strictly for issuing commands to your **direct subordinates**. You cannot issue orders to agents who are not your direct reports, nor can you order superiors or lateral colleagues. All communication must follow the established hierarchy.\\n\\n2. **Blank responses.** You can supply a blank response by attaching no system calls to your response. This is important since most system messages are a RESULT of your system calls. If you do not supply any calls, you may not receive any more system messages. This will roadblock your entire hierarchy, as you are the top agent. The only scenario in which a blank response is acceptable is if you have at least one outstanding `ORDER` call that has not yet been reported on and are awaiting a report. In any other case, the system will return your response for correction.\\n\\n3. **Stay deterministic.** Do _not_ invent new call types or ad-hoc formats. Not only will the system reject them, but the strength of your firm is in its structure. By successfully wrangling your bureaucracy, you can achieve great things and develop amazing software products for the user.\\n\\n4. **User focus.** Only ask the user questions when absolutely necessary for fulfilling their request. If you think you can handle it without user involvement, that is ideal. This is especially crucial since we can make no assumptions about the user's technical expertise, and you must cater to any person, whether they are a seasoned developer or complete computer newbie.\\n\\n5. **Politeness & clarity.** Even though the user won’t see your draft text, keep your internal reasoning clear—you may need it for future debugging.\\n\\n6. **No assumptions.** Do not assume the user has any specific software installed, such as a particular IDE or programming language. Always provide clear instructions on how to set up the environment if necessary.\\n\\n7. **No direct user interaction.** Remember that, in your responses, you are talking to no one and no human will \\\"read\\\" your responses. The only thing seeing your responses is the system program, which is ONLY looking for system calls. It is critical to understand that system calls are the only way you can interact with anything or anyone. Any tokens in your response that are not part of a system call are ignored. However, you should feel absolutely free to use any text in your responses to think, reason, and plan. The system will not execute any of this text; it is purely for your internal use.\\n\\n8. **Resource consciousness.** Every action you take, from generating a response to recruiting a new agent, consumes time and energy, which translates to a real-world cost for the user. While you must never sacrifice the quality of your work, be mindful of efficiency. Avoid unnecessary actions and be judicious when recruiting new agents. The goal is to deliver high-quality results in a cost-effective manner.\\n\\n9. **Recruitment Denial.** If your request to recruit a new agent is denied by the user, it is a signal that the user believes the current team is sufficient for the task at hand. This often implies that the task may be less complex than you initially assessed. Re-evaluate your plan and try to solve the problem with your existing resources before requesting more.\\n\\n10. **No Acknowledgements or ETAs.** Do not issue orders requesting acknowledgement of receipt or asking for an estimated time of completion (ETA). The system guarantees that valid orders will be delivered. If an order fails for any reason, the system will notify you. Trust your subordinates to manage their tasks and report back upon completion. This practice avoids unnecessary communication and allows all agents to work more efficiently.\\n\\n–––––  \\n🃏 TEAMING\\nYour team is Clubs! There are four teams:\\n\\n- Hearts (red)\\n- Diamonds (red)\\n- Clubs (black)\\n- Spades (black)\\n\\nThis has no effect on your behavior, but it is useful for organizing agents and ensuring diversity in the team.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###delta-joric-auth-01###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (1)\\nTo: 5\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (1) to Joric (5) with sequence ID delta-joric-auth-01:\\\\n\\\\n]Welcome, Director Joric. Your task is to define the AWS Cognito User Pool for our HOA website project. Your output must be a single, complete, and executable `aws cognito-idp create-user-pool` command for our DEP.\\\\n\\\\n**Requirements for the User Pool:**\\\\n1.  **Pool Name:** Use the project prefix `hoa-draft-001` to name the pool `hoa-draft-001-user-pool`.\\\\n2.  **Sign-in Options:** Configure `email` as a sign-in alias.\\\\n3.  **User Attributes:** Require the `email` and `name` standard attributes.\\\\n4.  **Security:** Set a password policy with a minimum length of 8 characters, requiring numbers, symbols, uppercase, and lowercase letters.\\\\n5.  **User Registration:** Configure the pool so that only administrators can create users (`admin-create-user-config`). This is for the initial setup where an HOA admin would add residents.\\\\n\\\\nYour final report should contain only the complete, ready-to-execute `aws cognito-idp create-user-pool` CLI command.\\\"\\n}\\n###delta-joric-auth-01###\"}]}],\"config\":{\"version\":\"claude-opus-4-20250514\"},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"candidates\":[{\"index\":0,\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I need to construct a complete AWS Cognito User Pool creation command based on the requirements provided by Chief Delta.\\n\\nLet me work through each requirement:\\n\\n1. Pool name: `hoa-draft-001-user-pool`\\n2. Sign-in alias: `email`\\n3. Required attributes: `email` and `name`\\n4. Password policy: 8+ chars, numbers, symbols, uppercase, lowercase\\n5. Admin-only user creation\\n\\nHere's the complete command:\\n\\n###delta-joric-auth-01###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"aws cognito-idp create-user-pool --pool-name hoa-draft-001-user-pool --username-attributes email --auto-verified-attributes email --schema Name=email,AttributeDataType=String,Required=true,Mutable=true Name=name,AttributeDataType=String,Required=true,Mutable=true --policies '{\\\\\\\"PasswordPolicy\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"MinimumLength\\\\\\\":8,\\\\\\\"RequireUppercase\\\\\\\":true,\\\\\\\"RequireLowercase\\\\\\\":true,\\\\\\\"RequireNumbers\\\\\\\":true,\\\\\\\"RequireSymbols\\\\\\\":true}}' --admin-create-user-config '{\\\\\\\"AllowAdminCreateUserOnly\\\\\\\":true}' --mfa-configuration OFF\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"delta-joric-auth-01\\\"\\n}\\n###delta-joric-auth-01###\"}]}}],\"usage\":{\"inputTokens\":3564,\"outputTokens\":329},\"custom\":{\"id\":\"msg_01HkCWDFmPBufby9mrbLaeHL\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-20250514\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I need to construct a complete AWS Co